,[2492,"Thin RSC composer — single `Promise.all` data load"],[5735,"Thin wrappers around scripts/lib/py/lab\\_topology.py: validate the declarative\nlab topology (ansible/inventory/lab.yaml), emit flat facts, and render the\ngenerated Ansible artifacts (hosts.ini, fabric group vars, netplan,\ncloud-init user-data). The topology file is the single source of truth —\n`manage.sh setup` (discovery wizard) rewrites it, rendering follows."],[2818,"Thinking / vision"],[2826,"Thinking / vision toggles and benchmark recipes"],[2009,"This document is the **canonical reference** for human contributors. Stack-specific addenda extend it:"],[5469,"This file is a primary source for auto-generated documentation.\nStructured comments here are extracted by docs/generate\\_shell\\_docs.py\nand appear in the built documentation site under Reference → Shell Commands & Helpers."],[3616,"This is **not** a ComfyUI beginner course. Still images and video on one Spark with Docker Compose: ez-comfy-stack."],[1125,"This is a **K3s lab** for 1–5 NVIDIA DGX Spark nodes — single node, 200 Gb/s QSFP pair, QSFP ring, or a Mikrotik CRS804 switch fabric (4–5 nodes). Workloads are Kubernetes Jobs and Deployments with explicit resources. Nothing heavy starts on reboot. Any of the Sparks can run the control plane."],[4614,"This is a lab, so re-assign the control plane freely — for example, when `spark0` is\noccupied with other experiments, run a 3-node ring on `spark1`, `spark2`, `spark3` with\n`spark1` as orchestrator, or a switch lab with `spark4` in charge. Cabling, netplan\nsubnets, and model placement do not care which node is the server; only the group\nmembership and the `k3s_control_plane` fact change."],[3117,"This is a safety rule in this repository specifically because the Resource Guard reads a JSON verdict produced by a shell utility, and that verdict gates heavy GPU workload starts. Before the boundary parser existed, the dashboard wrote `JSON.parse(stdout) as CapacityCheck`; a renamed key